<interrogationes unitas="arbor_syntaxis">

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I build new C tooling on arbor_syntaxis?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor_syntaxis_creare arbor_syntaxis_parsere" via="project-specs/generationes-praecedentes-archivandae.md silva/CLAUDE.md">You
      should not. arbor v1 (this recursive-descent parser) is the
      first of four generations: arbor v1, then arbor2 (hand-written
      GLR), then lapifex (generated LALR), then silva (GLR, generated
      tables). silva is the house C parser behind the LSP, the MCP
      server and the formatter. arbor v1 and its suites are parked
      for archival (ledger 01M0FT8AE7VP). Outside the arbor files
      themselves, its only consumers are lint/lint_probatio.c and
      tools/glr_quaestio.c, and neither is built. Read
      it to understand old tests or the archive. New work goes to
      silva.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I parse a C file into an arbor AST?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor_parsere_filum arbor_parsere_fontem arbor_syntaxis_parsere_fontem arbor_optiones_default">Use
      arbor_parsere_filum or arbor_parsere_fontem from arbor.h with
      NIHIL options. They run the preprocessor in HYBRID mode (follow
      includes, keep directives and Latin keyword text), install its
      keyword-macro and typedef tables on the parser, and return the
      token stream that the formatter needs. The default include path
      is "include" (arbor_optiones_default). The lower-level
      arbor_syntaxis_parsere_fontem uses the preprocessor's default
      PROCESSARE mode (macros expanded), installs no table, returns no
      lexemata and adds NO include path: only a quoted include that
      sits beside via_file is found, so a lib/ file that includes
      latina.h keeps its Latin keywords as plain identifiers.
      Preprocessor errors, such as an include that was not found,
      never reach res.errores. Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): a
      file containing a variadic prototype such as int f(int a, ...);
      never finishes parsing (no error, memory grows without
      limit).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does successus mean, and what is in errores?
    <responsum symbolum="ArborSyntaxisResultus ArborError arbor_error_formare arbor_syntaxis_parsere">successus
      is VERUM only when there is a root AND zero errors. With errors
      you usually still get a radix. Some errors also leave an ERROR
      node where the parser skipped ahead to just past a ';', or to a
      '}' or a statement keyword; others (a missing ')' or ';') only
      add an entry to errores. errores is a
      Xar of ArborError* POINTERS, despite the header comment's "Xar
      of ArborError": read it with *(ArborError**)xar_obtinere(...).
      via_file is always empty, even when you passed a path.
      arbor_error_formare gives "linea:columna: nuntius". errores is
      NIHIL when arbor_syntaxis_parsere gets a NIHIL parser or token
      Xar, and when lexing or preprocessing fails inside
      arbor_syntaxis_parsere_fontem, so check for NIHIL before you
      iterate.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I walk a declarator to find the name, pointers, array sizes and parameters?
    <responsum symbolum="ArborNodus arbor_nodus_imprimere">Iterate
      DECLARATOR's datum.genericum.liberi. The children come in
      order: zero or more POINTER nodes (qualifiers in
      datum.pointer.qualifiers), then an IDENTIFIER, or a nested
      DECLARATOR for a parenthesized declarator, or nothing for an
      abstract declarator, then ARRAY_DECLARATOR
      nodes (datum.array.size, NIHIL for []), then at most one
      FUNCTION_DECLARATOR. The FUNCTION_DECLARATOR's own
      genericum.liberi are PARAMETER_DECLARATION nodes; no
      PARAMETER_LIST node is ever built. f() has no children, and
      f(void) has one PARAMETER_DECLARATION whose only specifier is a
      void TYPE_SPECIFIER and whose declarator is NIHIL. The header's pointer.inner
      and array.inner fields are never set. Do not follow them.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what node kinds does the parser actually produce for commas, postfix ++, casts and parentheses?
    <responsum symbolum="ArborNodusGenus arbor_nodus_genus_nomen">Several
      enum members are never built. A comma operator is a
      BINARY_EXPRESSION with operator ARBOR_LEXEMA_COMMA, not
      COMMA_EXPRESSION. Postfix ++ and -- are UNARY_EXPRESSION with
      est_postfix VERUM, not POSTFIX_EXPRESSION. Initializers are the
      expression itself or an INITIALIZER_LIST, never INITIALIZER.
      The type-name in a cast or sizeof is a PARAMETER_DECLARATION.
      Parentheses get no node at all: see the trivia question.
      arbor_nodus_genus_nomen has no case for DIRECTIVE, so it
      returns "unknown" for directive nodes.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why are parentheses, commas and keyword spellings missing from the tree, and where did they go?
    <responsum symbolum="ArborTrivia arbor_formator_emittere_fidelis" via="lib/arbor_syntaxis.worklog.md">For
      byte-exact roundtrip. Punctuation and the keyword text as the
      token spells it (such as "si" for if, when the preprocessor kept
      it) are stored as SYNTHETIC trivia entries in the neighbouring
      nodes' trivia_ante or trivia_post, and
      arbor_formator_emittere_fidelis prints them back. A synthetic
      entry has linea ZEPHYRUM and est_commentum FALSUM, while real
      whitespace and comments carry their 1-based line. So
      est_commentum VERUM finds the comments, and linea ZEPHYRUM tells
      synthetic text from real whitespace. The trivia Xars hold
      ArborTrivia* pointers. They are often the very Xars owned by the
      tokens, and the parser appends to them, so the token stream's
      trivia changes as you parse.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can it parse variadic prototypes and K&R-style function definitions?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor_syntaxis_parsere ArborNodus">No.
      The parser never handles the ... token (ARBOR_LEXEMA_ELLIPSIS),
      so int f(int a, ...); fails with "Expectabatur )". The K&amp;R
      field datum.functio.old_params is always NIHIL ("not supported
      yet"). The worklog's open TODOs also list array type-names and
      function-pointer type-names in casts. Caution (audit 2026-10-18,
      unfixed): int f(int a, ...); does not fail with "Expectabatur )";
      the parse never returns and memory grows without limit.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does a statement like a * b; parse as a declaration?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor_syntaxis_ponere_typedef_nomina arbor_syntaxis_ponere_keyword_macros" via="lib/arbor_syntaxis.worklog.md">Typedef
      names come from four places: typedef declarations seen during
      the parse (one global table with no block scope), the table you
      merge in with arbor_syntaxis_ponere_typedef_nomina, keyword
      macros of kind ARBOR_LEXEMA_TYPEDEF_MACRO from
      arbor_syntaxis_ponere_keyword_macros, and a heuristic. The heuristic treats an unknown identifier as a type
      when the next token is '*' or another identifier. It was added
      so that PRESERVARE mode, which does not follow includes, does
      not loop on i32 x. Its cost is the classic ambiguity: a * b;
      becomes a declaration of b. A registered typedef name is taken
      as a type even after another type, so a block-scope int T; that
      shadows typedef int T; parses as specifiers only and declares
      nothing, with no error. Any identifier starting with __ in
      specifier position becomes an EXTENSION node.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what do literal nodes hold, are escapes decoded?
    <responsum symbolum="ArborNodus">textus
      is the raw source spelling (quotes, prefixes, suffixes and
      escapes all kept), interned and NOT NUL-terminated. For integers,
      valor is an i64 (unsigned in this house) computed with no
      overflow check, plus est_unsigned and est_long. For characters,
      valor decodes only \n \t \r \0 \\ and \'. Any other escape
      yields the character after the backslash: '\x41' gives 'x', not
      65, and '\012' gives 0, not 10. Adjacent string literals form
      ONE node whose textus is the first piece only. partes then
      lists every ArborLexema*.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I reuse one ArborSyntaxis for several files, and how deep can code nest?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor_syntaxis_creare arbor_syntaxis_parsere" via="lib/arbor_index.c">Reuse
      works: each arbor_syntaxis_parsere resets the position and the
      error list. The typedef table persists, though, so names from
      earlier files leak into later ones; make one parser per file,
      as arbor_index does. The depth counter goes up once per
      statement and once per compound block and stops at CCLVI with
      "Recursio nimis profunda", so about CXXVIII nested { } blocks
      (each is a statement AND a block) or CCLVI nested ifs.
      arbor_syntaxis_creare never initializes that counter and
      piscina_allocare does not zero memory, so it starts at whatever
      the piscina hands out. Expressions, parentheses, declarators and
      initializer lists have no limit, so very deep nesting there can
      overflow the C stack.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
